    "lawCode": "Amd §3011, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5643A\n\nSPONSOR: KLEIN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the public health law, in relation to\ndirecting the commissioner of health, in consultation with the commis-\nsioner of transportation and the New York state emergency medical\nservices council, to establish rules and regulations for ambulances\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to help ensure the safe transport of ambula-\ntory individuals that are being transported, as well as the safety of\nthe public during such transports. Currently, there are few rules and\nregulations for ambulances, specifically for drivers of such motor vehi-\ncles, to ensure the physical safety of such passengers and the public at\nlarge. This bill directs the commissioner of health, in consultation\nwith the commissioner of transportation and the New York State emergency\nmedical services council, to establish more oversight of such ambulance\noperators.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Amends Public Health Law section 3011 to a new subdivision\ndirecting the commissioner of health, in consultation with the commis-\nsioner of transportation and the state emergency medical services coun-\ncil, to establish rules and regulations for ambulances in order to\nensure the safety of passengers and the public at large.  Further speci-\nfies the context of such rules and regulations, stating that they shall\ninclude but shall not be limited to, the allowable number of consecutive\nhours an ambulance driver may work and the requisite training an ambu-\nlance driver shall receive prior to driving any ambulance.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nOn March 4, 2015, Janet Hickey of City Island was pronounced brain dead\nand later taken off life support after the ambulance she was riding in\ncrashed. The accident happened as paramedics took her to a rehab facili-\nty in Westchester County after brain surgery. The ambulance did not have\nlights and sirens on, but police said that the ambulance operator lost\ncontrol, slamming into a pole. A camera mounted inside the ambulance\ncaptured the ambulance driver falling asleep behind the wheel just\nbefore swerving off the roadway and striking a utility pole. Many EMT's\nand other emergency workers work long hours and it is not uncommon for\nthem to work many consecutive 12-hour shifts a week. There is currently\nnothing stopping a driver from working a double shift and further no\nrestrictions on work hours. This legislation establishes the necessary\noversight and regulation to prevent such traumatic and reckless acci-\ndents.\n\nThis bill accomplishes this by having the DOH, in conjunction with DOT\nand the New York State Emergency Medical Services Council to establish\nrules and standards to ensure that ambulance drivers do not work for so\nmany hours that they cannot operate an ambulance safely.  Further, to\nensure that all ambulance drivers have the requisite training needed to\nsafely operate such vehicles and can safely transport such passengers to\ntheir ultimate destination.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2017/2018 - Reported from Sen. Health Committee to Senate Floor\n2016 - Similar to S. 7799 / A. 10631 - Passed Senate.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n180 days after it shall have become law.\n",
